Let me design the header.

```c
#ifndef KV_INT_TOKEN_H
#define KV_INT_TOKEN_H

#include <stdint.h>

enum KvIntParse {
    KV_INT_OK,
    KV_INT_INVALID,
    KV_INT_RANGE,
};

// Разбор целого со строгостью std::from_chars: необязательный знак '-' (только для
// знаковых), хотя бы одна десятичная цифра, ведущие нули допустимы; '+'/пробельные
// символы/хвост - неверная форма. Выход за пределы целевого типа - KV_INT_RANGE.
static inline enum KvIntParse kv_parse_i32(const char* s, int32_t* out) {
    if (!s || !*s)
        return KV_INT_INVALID;
    int negative = 0;
    const char* p = s;
    if (*p == '-') {
        negative = 1;
        ++p;
    }
    if (*p < '0' || *p > '9')
        return KV_INT_INVALID;

    int64_t limit = negative ? -(int64_t)INT32_MIN : (int64_t)INT32_MAX;
    int64_t magnitude = 0;
    for (; *p; ++p) {
        if (*p < '0' || *p > '9')
            return KV_INT_INVALID;
        int digit = *p - '0';
        if (magnitude > (limit - digit) / 10)
            return KV_INT_RANGE;
        magnitude = magnitude * 10 + digit;
    }
    *out = (int32_t)(negative ? -magnitude : magnitude);
    return KV_INT_OK;
}
```

Check overflow guard: limit = INT32_MAX = 2147483647 for positive; for negative limit = 2147483648. Condition: magnitude > (limit - digit)/10 → overflow. E.g., magnitude=214748364, digit=7: (2147483647-7)/10 = 214748364 → not >, ok → 2147483647. digit=8: (2147483647-8)/10 = 214748363 → 214748364 > 214748363 → RANGE. Good.
For negative: 2147483648 allowed → magnitude=214748364, digit=8 → (2147483648-8)/10 = 214748364 → not > → ok → 2147483648 → -2147483648. Good. digit 9 → (2147483639)/10=214748363 → RANGE. Good.

But careful: after returning RANGE mid-scan, we don't validate trailing chars. from_chars returns out_of_range with ptr at the offending digit; callers map both to the same message. So classification difference only if the string is like "99999999999x" — from_chars: out_of_range (stops at digit 11 maybe) → C++ maps RANGE-ish; our C also RANGE. Same status. And if string is "2147483648x": C RANGE, C++ out_of_range. Same. Fine.

But there's a subtle mismatch: what if the string is "x9999999999" → INVALID both. Good.

Another subtlety: `-` followed by non-digit → INVALID. Both.

Empty → INVALID. from_chars: empty → invalid_argument (ec != {}) → callers treat as invalid. Good.

INT32_MIN needs <stdint.h> and INT32_MAX/INT32_MIN macros. Include <stdint.h>.

